    {
      "id": "2423547e868d638068d6d255b2fe77e24294de1d",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 808814383,
          "type": null,
          "body": "This is only a workaround. The problem is that pcapng.c assumes that the Interface Description Blocks (IDBs) follow the Section Header but I could not find that in the PCAPNG specification. In addition, pcapng_open seems to assume also that all IDBs (or at least 2, if more than 1 IDB exists) are at the start. I could not find this in the PCAPNG specification either.\n\nFixing the problem is not so obvious to me and seems to impact a lot of code.\n\nI have looked at different alternatives:\n1) Skip Custom Blocks in pcapng_open and assume IDBs follow. Parse those and hand them back.\n-    Problem: This would change the behavior for everyone and Custom Blocks would not be shown in Wireshark!?\n-    Problem: What happens, if not only Custom Blocks are in front of IDBs?\n2) Skip Custom Blocks, parse IDBs, and seek back to start.\n-    Problem: Since the interfaces are given back, applications might assume with later IDBs that they are different. This may lead to wrong interface IDs. :(\n-    Note: Not defining the Interface ID in the IDBs seems to be really a disadvantage.\n\nSince Wireshark seems to not have the problem, having this workaround in tshark seemed the best solution.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6878858,
            "username": "LarsVoelker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Dr. Lars Völker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/cccf2084a0438673e9e09f07ed0a5035f6e8815d3d5d888db0023a757c8dce8c?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/LarsVoelker"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-01-12T20:46:19.121Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-01-12T20:46:19.121Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 134881578,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 5853,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ef13656bbaaf5f525c537c05898ad9d4fd66e576",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 808823100,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> The problem is that pcapng.c assumes that the Interface Description Blocks (IDBs) follow the Section Header\n\nThe underlying problem is that the libwiretap API, and the way some programs expect it to work, doesn't do a good job of supporting file formats that don't have a single link-layer header type.  For those file formats, it usually starts out with an encapsulation type of \"unknown\" and typically tries to find an indication of a link-layer type in the file in the open process.  If it finds one or more, it switches to the link-layer type it finds or to \"per-packet\".\n\nThis probably needs to be redone, *along with the way programs that process capture files work*.  For example, a program that reads one or more files and writes a file may, when reading a file of that type, and writing a file of a type that has a single link-layer type indicated in the file header (e.g., a pcap file), may have to wait until a link-layer type is seen before creating the output file and, if it sees a packet with a different link-layer type, fail with \"sorry, this file can't handle multiple link-layer types\".\n\nI've made some changes in that direction, but there's more work to be done.\n\n> but I could not find that in the PCAPNG specification.\n\nIt's not in the pcapng specification because it's not a requirement of pcapng.  IDBs can appear anywhere as long as they're before any blocks that refer to that interface.  Consider a capture that starts out on two interfaces and, in the middle, starts capturing on a new interface that was created after the capture started.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-01-12T21:01:38.853Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-01-12T21:01:38.853Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 134881578,
